 ## Overview
-Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a ray segment defined by a near plane ($t_{min}$) and a far plane ($t_{max}$) for a specified number of points [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It serves as the foundational sampling strategy for coarse volume rendering passes, building on the ray origins and directions provided by the [Grid Ray Sampler](3930-grid-ray-sampler.md). It differs from its siblings by offering two distinct spacing modes—linear depth and linear disparity—allowing the renderer to control sample density relative to geometric depth or inverse depth, which is critical for handling scenes with varying depth scales [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a ray segment defined by a near plane ($t_{min}$) and a far plane ($t_{max}$) for a specified number of points [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of discretizing the continuous ray parameter space into a finite set of sample locations for volume rendering or neural rendering pipelines. It differs from its siblings by using uniform spacing in either linear depth or linear disparity (inverse depth) space, optionally perturbed by random jitter, whereas [Near-Middle-Far Ray Generation](6817-near-middle-far-ray-generation.md) uses a three-segment strategy and [Refined Ray Generation via PDF Sampling](6837-refined-ray-generation-via-pdf-sampling.md) uses importance sampling based on probability density functions.
 
